    topic: "The Aral Sea",
    passage: "When Dilnoza Karimova first stood on the dried-up seabed near Moynaq in 2009, she assumed the rusting ships in front of her were a tourist gimmick. Her guide, a retired fisherman named Bakhtiyor, quickly corrected her. Until the early 1960s, he explained, the Aral Sea was the fourth largest lake in the world, and Moynaq was a busy port that canned around one sixth of the Soviet Union's fish. Then the rivers feeding the sea, the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, were diverted to irrigate cotton fields, and the water began to retreat. By 1990 the shoreline had moved more than sixty kilometres away from the town, and the fishing industry had collapsed almost overnight.\n\nMany visitors, Bakhtiyor said, assume that drought alone caused the disaster. He disagreed strongly. Weather played a minor role, he argued; the real culprit was a decision made in offices far from the sea, where officials valued cotton harvests more than a fragile ecosystem. The consequences were not limited to unemployment. Salt and chemical dust blown from the exposed seabed damaged crops, and local doctors reported unusually high rates of throat and kidney illness. Rainfall in the region actually became less predictable, since the sea no longer softened the climate.\n\nDilnoza had expected the trip to be thoroughly depressing, yet she left with mixed feelings. In the northern part of the sea, known as the Small Aral, a dam completed in Kazakhstan in 2005 has allowed the water level to recover, and fishermen there now catch species that had vanished for decades. The southern part, by contrast, has almost entirely dried out, and scientists hardly expect it to return in anyone's lifetime. A new forest of saxaul shrubs, planted across about two million hectares, is helping to hold the salty soil in place, although experts warn that it cannot replace the lost water.\n\nTourism now supplies a modest income for Moynaq. Guesthouses offer simple rooms for around twenty dollars a night, and a small museum displays old photographs of the harbour. Bakhtiyor admits he is torn: he is pleased that people finally listen to his story, but he worries that visitors treat the ships as a photo opportunity rather than a warning. 'Take as many pictures as you like,' he tells each group, 'but please do not leave without asking yourself who made the decision, and who paid for it.' Dilnoza, who now writes about environmental policy, says that sentence changed the direction of her career.",
