    topic: "A glacier hike in Iceland",
    passage: "The alarm went off at a quarter to six, and by half past eight our group of thirteen hikers was standing at the foot of the Solheimajokull glacier in southern Iceland, adjusting the metal spikes, called crampons, that we had strapped to our boots. Our guide, a lean, softly spoken man named Gunnar Olafsson, checked every strap personally. 'Nobody walks on the ice without crampons,' he said, 'and nobody walks ahead of me, not even for a photograph.' I had expected a rather gentle stroll, but within ten minutes I understood why he was so strict: the surface was uneven, slippery and cut by narrow cracks that were hard to see.\n\nThe walk lasted about three hours, with a break for sandwiches beside a pool of astonishingly blue meltwater. Gunnar used the pause to explain what he has watched happen over fifteen years of guiding. When he started in 2011, the starting point for tours was a short walk from the car park; today it takes twenty minutes to reach the ice, because the glacier has retreated by roughly one kilometre. In some years it has shrunk by thirty metres in a single summer. He pointed to a pole on the hillside marking where the tongue of ice ended in 2005. It was now almost invisible against the grey rock far below us.\n\nWhat interested me most was Gunnar's attitude. I had assumed that a guide who earns his living from glacier tourists would play down the problem, or at least avoid depressing his customers. Quite the opposite. He told us bluntly that the glacier would probably be too small for safe tours within a few decades, and that he sometimes feels like a doctor taking visitors to say goodbye to a patient. A Canadian woman in our group argued that this kind of 'last chance tourism' is hypocritical, since flying to Iceland adds to the very warming that melts the ice. Gunnar did not disagree. 'The flight is the biggest part of the problem,' he admitted, 'but I have noticed that people who touch the ice tend to become more careful about their choices than people who only read about it.'\n\nI am still not sure who is right. Standing on the glacier, I felt something that statistics have never given me, and the following week I cancelled a planned weekend trip by plane. Yet I also know that one honest gesture does not offset a long-haul flight. On the walk back, Gunnar handed each of us a small bag and asked us to pick up any rubbish we saw; we collected almost nothing, which he said was the best result of the season so far.",
