    topic: "A plastic-free month",
    passage: "Last July, my friend Aziz bet me that I could not survive one month without buying anything wrapped in plastic. I accepted, mostly out of stubbornness, and I assumed the hardest part would be the kitchen. On the first evening I counted the plastic items in my flat and stopped at fifty-three, which included things I had never noticed: the lining of my coffee cups, the seals on tea bags, the tiny pieces of plastic in my chewing gum. I had always considered myself reasonably green because I recycled, but a little research showed that only a small fraction of the plastic collected in my city is actually turned into something new.\n\nThe first week was frustrating. The supermarket near my flat sells almost every vegetable in a plastic bag or tray, so I walked twenty minutes to the Chorsu bazaar, where I could buy tomatoes and cucumbers loose and put them in the cotton bags my grandmother had sewn years ago. Prices were lower there, which surprised me, although the shopping took roughly twice as long. Bread was easy, but cheese and yoghurt were nearly impossible, and I eventually learned to make yoghurt myself in a glass jar. The bathroom was a different story: I replaced shampoo with a solid bar and toothpaste with a tin of tablets, and I have to say that the tablets were awful.\n\nBy the third week I had a rhythm, yet an unexpected problem appeared. Friends began to treat my challenge as a performance. At a birthday dinner, a colleague named Kamola whispered that I was being a little extreme, and later that evening I found myself defending a plastic straw I had not even used. My brother Jasur was more direct. He argued that individual choices are almost meaningless when factories produce millions of tonnes of packaging each year, and that governments, not shoppers, should ban single-use plastic. I do not think he is entirely wrong, but I disagree that personal effort is pointless. When two neighbours asked where I bought my loose rice, I realised habits spread more quietly than laws do.\n\nAt the end of the month my household bin was lighter, though not empty: I had produced one small bag of plastic waste, mostly from medicine packaging, which I could not avoid. Aziz admitted I had won, and he paid for dinner. I will not keep every rule, because the tablets are going straight back to the shop. But I will continue buying vegetables at the bazaar, and I have promised to repeat the challenge next March, this time with Jasur as my partner, reluctant though he sounds.",
