    topic: "Culture shock abroad",
    passage: "When Sardor Ismoilov landed in Helsinki in August 2024 to begin a master's degree, he felt confident. He had studied English for ten years, read about Finnish education for months and even learned to say good morning in Finnish. For the first fortnight everything delighted him: the clean air, the punctual trams, the university library that stayed open until midnight. He later described this stage as the honeymoon period, a phase that almost every expatriate experiences and few expect to end.\n\nIt ended around the third month. By November the daylight lasted barely six hours, and Sardor began to notice small things that irritated him out of proportion. In his home city of Namangan, a neighbour would knock on the door with a plate of food at least once a week; in Helsinki he lived for ten weeks next to a man whose name he never learned. People in the lift stared at the floor, and a classmate who had seemed friendly on Monday walked past him on Wednesday without a greeting. Sardor concluded that Finns were cold and uninterested in foreigners, and he phoned his mother twice a day, which he now describes as a mistake.\n\nThe turning point came from a Finnish tutor, Elina, who explained that silence in her culture is not rudeness but respect. 'If I leave you alone, I am saying that I trust you to manage,' she told him. Sardor admits he did not believe this at first. Yet when he joined a volleyball club that met on Thursday evenings, he noticed that team-mates who spoke little during the day shouted encouragement during matches and invited him to the sauna afterwards. 'It was a shock to be so naked among people I hardly knew,' he laughs, 'and it was also the day I understood them.'\n\nResearchers who study the subject often point out that culture shock is rarely caused by dramatic differences such as food or religion, which people prepare for. It is the invisible habits, such as how close to stand, when to speak, or whether to make small talk, that cause the most stress, because nobody explains them. Sardor agrees, though he adds one qualification: he thinks the effort must come from both sides. Three months ago he began organising a monthly dinner where new students cook dishes from home and invite Finnish classmates. The first dinner attracted nine people, last month's attracted twenty-six, and Elina arrived early with a bag of rye bread.",
