    topic: "A wildlife reserve in Kenya",
    passage: "At five-thirty on a cold July morning, ranger Wanjiru Kamau was already scanning the grass near the Mara River with binoculars, listening to the radio for news of the lions. Wanjiru works in a private conservancy next to the Maasai Mara National Reserve in Kenya, and she is quick to point out that the difference between the two is more than a fence. In the national reserve, visitors pay a fee to the government and minibuses often crowd around a single animal. In the conservancy, the land is leased from around eight hundred Maasai families, who receive a monthly payment as long as they limit the number of cattle and tourist beds on their land.\n\nThe rules sound strict. No more than five vehicles may approach an animal at once, nobody may leave the car except at designated spots, and night drives are allowed only for guests staying inside the conservancy. A visit costs about three times as much as in the national reserve, which Wanjiru defends without embarrassment. 'You can pay a smaller price and see fifty cars around a leopard,' she says, 'or you can pay more and see the leopard.' Not every guest agrees. A Spanish family I met at breakfast complained that the price seemed unreasonable, though they admitted that their children had watched a lioness teach her cubs to hunt for forty minutes without a single other vehicle in sight.\n\nThe results are visible. According to the conservancy's records, the number of lions has risen from nine to fourteen over the past decade, and cheetah sightings, once rare, now happen weekly. Wanjiru credits the improvement to something other than tourism alone. Because families are paid to share land, young Maasai men have stopped hunting lions in revenge for lost cattle, and instead several of them train as trackers who warn herders when predators approach. She herself grew up three villages away and spent her childhood being told to fear lions; now she earns a salary studying them.\n\nStill, she refuses to describe the model as perfect. Droughts push cattle toward the protected grass, and during the last dry season, nearly four hundred animals were found grazing illegally inside the boundary. Elephants occasionally destroy crops, and compensation can take months to arrive. 'A conservancy works only while people believe they are winning,' she says, 'and belief is easier to lose than lions.' As we drove back at dusk, a cheetah crossed the track ahead of us, and the guide switched off the engine. For a minute, nobody spoke.",
