    topic: "A street food tour",
    passage: "I am normally suspicious of organised food tours, so when my cousin Farrukh insisted I join one during my week in Mexico City, I agreed only because he had already paid. The tour began at six in the evening outside a metro station in the Roma district, and our guide, a cheerful university lecturer named Lucia Herrera, announced that we would be eating at six stalls over four hours. 'Nobody should leave hungry,' she said, 'and nobody should leave without learning something.' The price was thirty-five dollars, which seemed expensive until I realised how much food it included.\n\nOur first stop was a woman selling tamales from a steaming metal pot on the pavement. Lucia explained that tamales, parcels of corn dough cooked in leaves, are older than the Spanish arrival, and that the dish changes completely every few streets depending on the filling. I chose one with green sauce and chicken, and my cousin chose sweet pineapple. Both were better than anything I had eaten in the restaurants where I had spent the previous three evenings. Our second stop was a taco stand, where a man sliced pork from a spinning cone of meat with the speed of a surgeon. Lucia reminded us that Mexicans do not eat hard, crunchy shells; those are an invention of restaurants abroad.\n\nMany of the guests, including me, had arrived afraid of getting ill. A nervous Australian man asked whether street food was safe, and Lucia answered with a surprising argument. In her experience, stomachs suffer more from buffets, where food sits for hours at an unsafe temperature, than from stalls where meat is cooked in front of the customer. 'Watch for a queue of local people,' she advised. 'A long queue means fresh food and a cook who cannot afford to disappoint.' I noticed that the busiest stalls were indeed the cleanest, and none of our group of eleven was ill the following day, although one vegetarian, Madina, found that most of the stops offered her little more than beans and cheese.\n\nThe final stop was the most memorable: a tiny cart selling churros dipped in thick chocolate, which we ate sitting on a kerb at ten o'clock while a street musician played accordion. I had begun the evening as a sceptic. By the end, I was convinced that the best way to understand a city is not through its monuments but through what its people eat standing up. Farrukh smiled and said he had been right all along. I conceded the point, on the condition that he pay for tomorrow's lunch as well.",
