    topic: "Traditional crafts in Rishtan",
    passage: "In the town of Rishtan, in the Fergana Valley, the family workshop of master potter Abdulaziz Yusupov stands behind a plain blue gate that visitors often walk past. Inside, six people sit among shelves of drying plates, each decorated with the deep turquoise and cobalt patterns for which the region has been famous for roughly eight hundred years. Abdulaziz belongs to the seventh generation of his family to work with clay, and he is quick to say that the secret of Rishtan ceramics is not a technique but a combination of ingredients. The local red clay is unusually fine, and the glaze is made from ishkor, a plant ash gathered from desert shrubs, which produces the characteristic glossy blue when fired.\n\nA large ceremonial plate takes about three weeks to make, because the clay must dry slowly, then be fired twice, with a pause for painting in between. Rushing any stage, Abdulaziz says, produces cracks that appear only when a customer takes the plate home. He once threw away an entire kiln load of forty pieces because the temperature rose too quickly. 'Money is not the only thing you lose,' he told me. 'You lose a week of other people's trust.' Customers come from as far as Japan and Germany, and a few ask him to explain each pattern. A pomegranate means abundance, he says, while a moon shape represents the passing of time.\n\nThe business, however, has not been easy. During the 1970s, factories supplied cheap, uniform plates to every household, and demand for handmade work almost disappeared. Abdulaziz's grandfather kept the workshop alive by repairing broken dishes. Today the opposite problem exists: tourists want souvenirs, but many look for the lowest price, and machine-made copies imported from other countries now sell in the capital for a quarter of what a handmade piece costs. Abdulaziz does not blame buyers entirely. He argues that most people cannot tell the difference unless someone shows them, which is why he insists that every visitor tries throwing a bowl on the wheel for ten minutes. 'After that,' he laughs, 'they stop asking why it costs so much.'\n\nThe future of the craft depends on young people, and the master is cautiously hopeful. Four years ago, only his own son was learning the trade; now there are five apprentices, including a fourteen-year-old girl named Sevara, who arrives after school each afternoon. The workshop does not pay high wages, but it offers something many jobs cannot: skill that cannot be replaced by a machine. Abdulaziz hopes to build a small school next to the workshop within three years, funded partly by visitors. Whether tradition survives, he suggests, will depend less on pride than on whether a young person can earn a decent living from it.",
