    topic: "A train across Central Asia",
    passage: "The train from Tashkent to Almaty is not fast, and I chose it for exactly that reason. After years of flying between Central Asian capitals in ninety minutes and arriving with no sense of having travelled, I wanted to see the distance. The journey takes about twenty hours, though the timetable promises nineteen, and my second-class compartment held four berths, two of which were occupied by a retired engineer, Mr Sultanbekov, and his granddaughter Aigerim, a nine-year-old with an extraordinary appetite for questions.\n\nThe first four hours were spent crossing the irrigated plains south of the Syr Darya, a landscape of cotton fields and straight canals that, from the window, resembled a green and brown chessboard. Mr Sultanbekov spread a cloth across the table and unpacked boiled eggs, flatbread, cucumbers and a jar of apricot jam, insisting that I join them. I had brought sandwiches, but it would have been rude to refuse. The tea, served by the carriage attendant from a huge samovar, cost the equivalent of one dollar for five glasses, and she refilled them whenever the conversation grew lively.\n\nThe border crossing was less romantic. At about two in the morning, the train stopped for almost three hours, officials in uniform entered every compartment, and Aigerim, who had been asleep, woke up and announced that she was not afraid of anything. Mr Sultanbekov smiled and said that he had made this journey in 1985 and again in 2004; the first time, he recalled, the border did not exist, and the second time it took six hours. Whether the delay is shorter or longer now depends, he said, on the mood of the officers and the age of the paperwork. I found his patience contagious.\n\nBy morning we were crossing the steppe of southern Kazakhstan, a vast, flat landscape of grass and distant mountains whose snow glowed pink. I realised that I had hardly looked at my phone since leaving Tashkent. Some travellers, I know, regard the train as a waste of time, and I understand their position: a flight would have cost less than a hundred dollars and saved a full day. Yet I would argue that the day was not lost. I had spoken to two strangers, read half a novel, and watched the continent change colour. When we arrived in Almaty two hours behind schedule, Aigerim hugged me and gave me her drawing of the train, which now hangs above my desk. I do not expect to travel this way every year, but I know I shall do it again.",
