    topic: "A language exchange trip to Spain",
    passage: "When Nigora Yusupova, a twenty-three-year-old economics graduate from Samarkand, booked a six-week language exchange trip to Salamanca last summer, she expected to improve her Spanish. She did not expect to discover how much she did not know about her own language. The programme paired each foreign student with a local volunteer, and Nigora's partner was Marta, a bright, impatient law student who wanted to learn Russian before an internship in Warsaw. They agreed on a simple rule: thirty minutes in Spanish, thirty minutes in Russian, and no switching to English, even when the conversation became difficult.\n\nThe first week was exhausting. Nigora's school Spanish was grammatically accurate but painfully slow, and she often searched for words while Marta waited with polite silence. She was also startled by how fast people spoke in the bars and markets. 'They do not pronounce the ends of words,' she complained, 'and nobody warned me.' Yet she found that Marta's correction style helped more than any textbook. Instead of stopping her at every error, Marta noted the mistakes in a small notebook and discussed only the three most important ones at the end. On the fourth day, Nigora announced proudly to a room of amused strangers that she was embarazada, meaning embarrassed. In Spanish, the word means pregnant.\n\nThe most valuable lessons, however, were cultural. Nigora had assumed that being direct was impolite, as it often is at home, and she softened every request with long apologies. Marta found this endearing but confusing. In Spain, she explained, a short question is not rude; it is efficient. Meanwhile, teaching Russian forced Marta to ask Nigora why verbs behave as they do, and Nigora, who had never studied grammar formally, struggled to explain. By the fifth week, she was buying a Russian grammar book for herself, laughing that she had come to Spain to study Russian.\n\nNigora is convinced that immersion works faster than classroom study, but only under one condition: that learners avoid spending time with compatriots. Five of the nine students in her group, she noticed, ate every meal together and spoke Russian or Uzbek all evening. Their progress, she says, was visibly slower. When she left in August, she and Marta promised to continue their weekly calls, and Marta sent her a tiny notebook, identical to her own, with a single sentence on the first page: 'Your mistakes are my favourite part of your Spanish.' Nigora plans to return next year, and this time she hopes to bring a friend who will agree to sit at a different table.",
